WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.510
Our friends, Karen and Noah Vanna Clawson

2
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.100
have taken our show on the road,

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.330
cruising across the country,

4
00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.830
shining a light on these amazing people

5
00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.650
and places along the way.

6
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.250
And they recently made a pit stop in Albuquerque,

7
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.750
New Mexico, where they rattled up some new friends.

8
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:21.449
Take a look at this special edition of Barry Moore Tours.

9
00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:24.616
(bright upbeat music)

10
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:29.280 line:15%
Hey Drew,

11
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.680 line:15%
we're in Albuquerque, New Mexico,

12
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.770 line:15%
at the American International Rattlesnake Museum

13
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.080 line:15%
and home of the world's largest collection

14
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.840 line:15%
of rattlesnake species.

15
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.252 line:15%
We gonna slither on in it, take a look.

16
00:00:41.252 --> 00:00:43.190 line:15%
(bright music)

17
00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.880 line:15%
So we're here with Bob Meyers, the founder and director

18
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.500 line:15%
of the American International Rattlesnake Museum.

19
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.570 line:15%
Bob. It's great to meet you.

20
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:50.403 line:15%
Thanks for hosting us.

21
00:00:50.403 --> 00:00:51.236 line:15%
Good to meet you, dude.

22
00:00:51.236 --> 00:00:52.400 line:15%
So tell us about the museum.

23
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.280 line:15%
When was it founded?

24
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.540 line:15%
The Rattlesnake Museum was founded in 1990, 31 years ago.

25
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.680 line:15%
So this is a huge tourist hotspot obviously.

26
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.740 line:15%
Oh, we get in excess of 50,000 people every year.

27
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.090 line:15%
How many rattlesnakes are there here?

28
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.300 line:15%
Oh, we have 34 different species

29
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.110 line:15%
and subspecies of rattlesnakes.

30
00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.472 line:15%
We have an albino here named, "Drew" of all things.

31
00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:14.760 line:15%
No.

32
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:15.626 line:15%
Should we go take a look?

33
00:01:15.626 --> 00:01:17.070 line:15%
Come on.
All right, let's do it.

34
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:17.903 line:15%
Thanks.

35
00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:20.760 line:15%
This is Drew, the Western Diamondback rattlesnake.

36
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.290 line:15%
She happens to be a blonde.

37
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.530 line:15%
She is Alpine.

38
00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.790 line:15%
Hi Drew.

39
00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:25.650 line:15%
Is she dangerous?

40
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.930 line:15%
She's dangerous because of her eventual size

41
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.300 line:15%
the length of those fangs,

42
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.550 line:15%
the quantity of venom that she sprays.

43
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.450 line:15%
What's the biggest thing people get wrong

44
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:35.660 line:15%
about rattlesnakes?

45
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.330 line:15%
Probably top on the list is the old wise tale

46
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.197 line:15%
that you can tell the age of a rattlesnake

47
00:01:41.197 --> 00:01:44.090 line:15%
by counting the segments on the rattle.

48
00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.040 line:15%
The truth is they get a rattle segment

49
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.913 line:15%
every time they shed their skin.

50
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.550 line:15%
Let me introduce you to "AJ" Adam Jr.

51
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.760 line:15%
He is an Eastern Diamondback rattlesnake,

52
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.870 line:15%
the largest species of rattlesnake in the world,

53
00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.866 line:15%
Found on the Southeastern part of the country.

54
00:02:02.866 --> 00:02:04.637 line:15%
How long is this one?

55
00:02:04.637 --> 00:02:07.740 line:15%
"AJ" is six feet, two inches now.

56
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:08.840 line:15%
Oh, my gosh.

57
00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.520 line:15%
It's beautiful.

58
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.530 line:15%
Absolutely beautiful.

59
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.480 line:15%
Thank you for letting us see him Bob.

60
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.430 line:15%
Absolutely.

61
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:17.166 line:15%
(audience applauding)

62
00:02:17.166 --> 00:02:19.407 line:15%
I really love that snake called "Drew."

63
00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.200 line:15%
I really do like that.

64
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.340 line:15%
I know that I'm always seeming like so happy and optimistic

65
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.150 line:15%
but I have a stinger.

66
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.500 line:15%
We all do.

67
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.090 line:15%
Nobody likes to be messed with.

68
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.670 line:15%
And that's the funny thing,

69
00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.720 line:15%
is when you're always so optimistic,

70
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.420 line:15%
people think you don't have like a spine

71
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.556 line:15%
and a dark side.

72
00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:40.306 line:15%
(dramatic music)

